{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/797726-arts-realms-teaser","id":797726,"title":"Arts & Realms teaser","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-01T02:25:15-08:00","id":797726,"title":"Arts & Realms teaser","content":"I share here what I commented on the KS comments section:<br /> <br /> I have to admit: I have very mixed feelings about this excerpt. As a C:tD fan since its first release, as someone who was involved, albeit in a very indirect way, in the Book of Glamour, I can't help but feel that some of the original spirit of the game is being abandoned.<br /> <br /> I *do* like some of the ideas presented here. But others are quite worrisome in my eyes.<br /> <br /> I comment here, since Matt said that this was still in development, and maybe our comments will be taken into account.<br /> <br /> First: capitalization. Changelings, Nobles and Commoners are written with capitals. It wasn't like this before, and I sure think that it was a good idea. Too many capitalized words in a text make for a very annoying reading. Plus, it tends to imply that these words have a specific meaning when, in fact, they don't: they have the same meaning as in the everyday language, only the context to which they are applied change. I hope the whole book is not written like that. I notice that Glamour is not capitalized, when maybe it ought to be.<br /> <br /> One sentence bothers me:<br /> <br /> &quot;Changelings\u2019 ability to weave glamour to marvel[l]ous and terrifying ends gave rise to legends of the faeries who steal mortal memories, bound over mountains, and reshape the world into an effigy of their forgotten dreams. &quot;<br /> <br /> The subject of this sentence should not be &quot;Changelings' ability&quot; but &quot;fae&quot; or &quot;Kithain&quot;. The sentence implies that changelings' ability to weave Glamour gave rise to legends, when, in fact, the legends rose in old times, *before* changelings existed (at least those we know and play in C:tD). This is a very small detail, I know, but the Devil...<br /> <br /> Finally, the cantrip system:<br /> <br /> My greatest regret would be that cantrip difficulty would not be based any more upon the caster's or the target's Banality (whichever is higher) : it made for a very simple, and very meaningful system.<br /> <br /> Cantrips are the ability to weave Glamour into desired effects. As all things with Glamour, Arts rest on belief. Belief, in the World of Darkness, is impaired by disbelief, aka Banality. So it made perfect sense that to successfully enchant someone, a changeling had to overcome her Banality, and when a changeling tried to enchant an object or a place, it was his own Banality (or the highest Banality of witnesses).<br /> <br /> The excerpt here doesn't delve into details, and we don't know if the chimerical/ Wyrd dichotomy of cantrips has been maintained. I doesn't look like it has, since every cantrip costs 1 Glamour. I regret that. I like the idea that when casting chimerical cantrips on enchanted beings/ objects, cantrips didn't cost any Glamour. It made for a kind of easy-going fae magic when used among fae. Instead, here we see bonus modifiers, which, I think, changes the implications behind the magic.<br /> <br /> As for the supposedly redundancy between the Primal Art and Nature Realm, I just don't see it. However, the aim to make all Arts and compatible with all Realms is a very good, one that Chris Howard had emphasized to all those who contributed to the Book of Glamour.<br /> <br /> Bunks can be multiple. Okay, though I don't really understand how it works. I like -- no, no, I *love* -- Bunks: they're the signal that fae magic is chaotic, never totally controlled, and a little silly, just like the fae are. It makes for extraordinary (in the truest sense of the word) scenes in a game. Suddenly, in a street, your pooka bard launches an improvised poem, much like Cyrano de Bergerac in the first scene of the play, while weaving his cantrip, and fighting off his opponent with his chimerical sword. Imagine the reaction of bystanders. Some would be delighted, others would be alarmed; some might even call the police. Bunks made me change the way I looked at people who behaved erratically in the street. Tramps, for instance. When a tramp passes by, mumbling incoherent things, and suddenly shouts at a bus shelter, despite the fact that the rational me guesses at the tragic misery and suffering behind this behavior, the Changeling gamer in me can also smile and feel not repulsed by this man but sympathy.<br /> <br /> As a said, I don't understand how Bunks work, now. I do like the idea of making bunks more lasting (this is something I have long encouraged among my players). Yet, I liked the Bunk ratings in the 2nd edition (from -1 to -5 on cantrip difficulty), and, truth to tell, I liked a lot the idea that Bunks were drawn randomly in the 1st edition (though the whole system didn't work really well).<br /> <br /> As for Unleashing, I have to admit, that I was never really fond of it in DA: Fae (as I didn't really like the whole DA: Fae: I liked the background of the game, or parts of it, but I disliked the magic and I disliked some of its history as well as the fact that there was no continuity between DA: Fae and C:tD).<br /> <br /> Here I like the idea that Unleashing is tied up with Nightmares. I interpret this as a sign that the rise of the Fomorians has enabled fae to use Unleashing. As for the rest, I am not sure what to think. I need to know more.<br /> <br /> Well, I think that's it. I hope this was not too harsh, or critical. I am aware that no one cares about anyone's opinions as they often appear to be self-centred (I hope I am not here). But maybe some people will find this noteworthy. Again, it makes me wonder as to why Christopher Howard was not consulted for his work on Book of Glamour, as he did a marvellous job.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3747,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3747-mattboggan","user":"mattboggan","date":". 01-01-2016, 08:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3747-mattboggan","name":"mattboggan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3747_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-01T11:10:48-08:00","id":797876,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the old ratings for Bunks seem more elegant than the current system, although I like the fact it specifies the elements of a Bunk.<br /> <br /> Unleashing is good but unclear. Is it limited by a character's rating in an Art? E.g., can someone with Wayfare 1 only create effects similar to Hopscotch? Or can they do anything related to travel and movement?<br /> <br /> The fixed difficulty is nice and easy, although fixing it to Banality felt more intuitive. Since most humans have a Banality rating of 7 anyway, it wouldn't make much mechanical difference (I always dropped the +4 bit anyway).<br /> <br /> I'd like to see a sample Art next.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 01-02-2016, 12:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-01T14:05:56-08:00","id":797964,"title":"","content":"Also, I second the notion that Glamour-free chimerical cantrips are essential. It's a big part of the setting that fae can do astonishing things that only they can see.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-02T08:35:25-08:00","id":798271,"title":"","content":"A certain amount of casual magic is really part of the fun for these kind of games. Really, just like my Mage character should be able to say, light the candles in her sanctum with a wave of her hand without it really requiring rolls or expenditures, my changeling characters should be able to do similar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-02T09:35:07-08:00","id":798295,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/797726-arts-realms-teaser?p=798271#post798271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A certain amount of casual magic is really part of the fun for these kind of games. Really, just like my Mage character should be able to say, light the candles in her sanctum with a wave of her hand without it really requiring rolls or expenditures, my changeling characters should be able to do similar.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. That was always something I liked about Changeling.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}